; expect 42
; A bare string literal is a `Str`: it evaluates to the address of a
; static { len, bytes } header, so `strLen` is a load of a
; compile-time-known length rather than the `cstrLen` scan
; `strFromLit` performs.
;
; This case is what proves stage1 implements the same language as
; stage0 rather than merely reproducing itself. The bootstrap fixpoint
; cannot show it: stage2 and stage3 agree byte-for-byte whether or not
; `emitStrExpr` resolves to the header or to the bytes, because both
; are built from the same source. Getting it wrong is silent - the
; length word would be read out of the first eight bytes of the text.
(import Str)
(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (let ((s "hello"))
    (+ (strLen s) 37)))
